// eprint, over the same serial port as print.
//
// A kernel has one place to write, so eprint writes there rather than being
// refused at compile time: code shared with a hosted build keeps building, and
// a port with no buffer keeps the lines in the order they were written. Like
// the hosted eprint it flushes what print has written first, which here is
// nothing to wait for, so the two share a port without sharing a body.

eprint(string s) {
  flush();
  put(s);
  com1_out(10);
} return void;
